    <h2>Key Nutrients in a Prenatal Vitamin</h2>

    <h3>Folic Acid (400-800 mcg)</h3>
    <p>Folic acid is the synthetic form of folate, a B vitamin that reduces the risk of neural tube defects (NTDs) such as spina bifida. The neural tube forms and closes by week 28, often before many women know they are pregnant. This is why ACOG recommends that all women who could become pregnant take 400-800 mcg of folic acid daily.</p>

    <h3>Iron (27 mg)</h3>
    <p>Iron supports the increased blood volume during pregnancy and helps prevent anemia. Your blood volume increases by about 50% during pregnancy, and your baby is also building their own iron stores.</p>

    <h3>Calcium (1,000 mg)</h3>
    <p>Calcium supports the development of the baby's bones and teeth. If you do not get enough calcium in your diet, your body will take calcium from your bones to support the baby.</p>

    <h3>Vitamin D (600 IU)</h3>
    <p>Vitamin D supports calcium absorption and bone health. It also plays a role in immune function. Many women are deficient in vitamin D, especially those who live in northern climates or have darker skin.</p>

    <h3>DHA (200-300 mg)</h3>
    <p>DHA (docosahexaenoic acid) is an omega-3 fatty acid that supports the baby's brain and eye development. It is found in fatty fish and algae-based supplements.</p>

    <h3>Iodine (220 mcg)</h3>
    <p>Iodine is essential for the baby's thyroid function and brain development. Mild iodine deficiency is common in the United States.</p>

    <h3>Choline (450 mg)</h3>
    <p>Choline supports the baby's brain development and helps prevent neural tube defects. Most prenatal vitamins do not contain enough choline, so dietary sources (eggs, meat, fish) are important.</p>

    <h2>When to Start Taking Prenatal Vitamins</h2>

    <p>ACOG recommends starting a prenatal vitamin at least one month before conception. Because many pregnancies are unplanned, it is best for all women who could become pregnant to take a prenatal vitamin daily.</p>

    <p>If you are already pregnant, start taking a prenatal vitamin as soon as you find out. The first trimester is a critical period for neural tube development.</p>

    <h2>Choosing a Prenatal Vitamin</h2>

    <p>Not all prenatal vitamins are created equal. Here is what to look for:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Folic acid:</strong> 400-800 mcg</li>
      <li><strong>Iron:</strong> 27 mg</li>
      <li><strong>Calcium:</strong> 200-300 mg (most women need additional calcium from diet or a separate supplement)</li>
      <li><strong>Vitamin D:</strong> 600 IU</li>
      <li><strong>DHA:</strong> 200-300 mg</li>
      <li><strong>Iodine:</strong> 220 mcg</li>
      <li><strong>Choline:</strong> 450 mg (often not included in prenatal vitamins)</li>
    </ul>

